00:01:50.164 --> 00:01:50.264
base.

00:01:50.798 --> 00:01:50.998
&gt;&gt; Molly jong-fast, it seems

00:01:51.265 --> 00:01:52.800
trump's positioning his

00:01:53.066 --> 00:01:53.867
presidential run as kind of a

00:01:54.134 --> 00:01:54.902
revenge tour.

00:01:55.169 --> 00:01:56.970
I think we have some sound from

00:01:57.237 --> 00:01:57.971
trump's speech.

00:01:58.238 --> 00:01:59.006
I think this is one that is

00:01:59.273 --> 00:02:00.174
going to be making a lot of

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:00.808
headlines.

00:02:00.874 --> 00:02:01.108
Let's listen.

00:02:01.775 --> 00:02:04.945
&gt;&gt; In 2016, I declared I am

00:02:05.212 --> 00:02:05.913
your voice.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.884
Today, I aim to clear, I am

00:02:11.151 --> 00:02:12.986
your lawyer, I am your justice.

00:02:13.253 --> 00:02:14.121
And for those who have been

00:02:14.388 --> 00:02:15.889
wronged and betrayed, I am your

00:02:16.156 --> 00:02:16.356
retribution.

00:02:16.824 --> 00:02:17.057
I am your retribution.

00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:20.828
&gt;&gt; I am your retribution.

00:02:21.061 --> 00:02:22.095
He said it twice, Molly

00:02:22.362 --> 00:02:22.830
jong-fast.

00:02:25.966 --> 00:02:28.001
&gt;&gt; Look, this guy is out for

00:02:28.268 --> 00:02:28.368
blood.

00:02:28.836 --> 00:02:29.169
And I think the problem with

00:02:29.436 --> 00:02:30.938
all of these more normal

00:02:31.205 --> 00:02:33.173
calculus is about Ron Desantis

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:34.174
is that trump is not a normal

00:02:34.441 --> 00:02:34.842
candidate.

00:02:34.942 --> 00:02:36.944
So, trump will not do what mark

00:02:37.211 --> 00:02:38.145
aruba did or Ted Cruz did and

00:02:38.412 --> 00:02:39.079
go quietly.

00:02:39.913 --> 00:02:41.248
So, I think that, for a lot of

00:02:41.515 --> 00:02:43.050
these Republicans who think

00:02:43.317 --> 00:02:45.018
they can swap him out, I am not

00:02:45.285 --> 00:02:46.186
sure how that works when you

00:02:46.453 --> 00:02:48.021
have someone who really is

00:02:48.288 --> 00:02:49.256
comfortable running as a third

00:02:49.523 --> 00:02:51.959
party candidate or just

00:02:52.226 --> 00:02:53.126
destroying the party on his way

00:02:53.393 --> 00:02:53.961
out.

00:02:54.228 --> 00:02:56.930
I mean, remember, trump is not

00:02:57.197 --> 00:02:57.531
a Republican per se.

00:02:58.098 --> 00:03:00.067
He is whatever that is.

00:03:00.934 --> 00:03:03.070
And I don't think his heart is

00:03:03.337 --> 00:03:04.238
with the Republican party.

00:03:04.504 --> 00:03:07.908
And I do not believe he will

00:03:08.175 --> 00:03:08.942
support whoever is the

00:03:09.209 --> 00:03:09.376
candidate.

00:03:09.643 --> 00:03:11.945
&gt;&gt; And shermichael singleton,

00:03:12.212 --> 00:03:13.146
what do you make of the state

00:03:13.413 --> 00:03:13.547
of CPAC?

00:03:13.814 --> 00:03:14.348
Is it the same conference that

00:03:14.882 --> 00:03:15.082
used to be?

00:03:15.082 --> 00:03:17.050
I remember covering it six or

00:03:17.317 --> 00:03:17.584
seven years ago.

00:03:17.918 --> 00:03:20.020
And it just strikes me as a

00:03:20.287 --> 00:03:22.022
very different organization.

00:03:22.289 --> 00:03:23.123
Mike pence, not there.

00:03:23.891 --> 00:03:26.059
Ron Desantis, not there.

00:03:26.326 --> 00:03:27.127
It's a different CPAC now.

00:03:27.928 --> 00:03:29.296
Trump has a lot of people in

00:03:30.063 --> 00:03:30.397
that room right now.

00:03:30.898 --> 00:03:32.065
But some of the speeches

00:03:32.332 --> 00:03:33.934
earlier on in the conference

00:03:34.201 --> 00:03:35.202
had me thinking whether CPAC

00:03:35.469 --> 00:03:36.336
should be held at the holiday

00:03:36.904 --> 00:03:36.937
inn in crystal city.

00:03:40.173 --> 00:03:41.408
&gt;&gt; Jim, it's evidently a

00:03:41.675 --> 00:03:44.311
different CPAC then it was when

00:03:44.578 --> 00:03:45.479
I used to go years and years

00:03:45.746 --> 00:03:46.079
ago.

00:03:46.346 --> 00:03:47.447
&gt;&gt; The sort of intellectual-ism

00:03:47.714 --> 00:03:50.183
that, once upon a time, to find

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:52.185
a Republican party because of

00:03:52.452 --> 00:03:56.189
individuals like Karl rove,

00:03:56.456 --> 00:03:57.324
intellectually speaking, or --

00:03:58.158 --> 00:03:59.359
minions of what it will --

00:04:00.193 --> 00:04:02.262
national review, which lowery

00:04:02.529 --> 00:04:02.562
--

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.197
those guys really are not

00:04:04.464 --> 00:04:05.432
activists and participants in

00:04:05.699 --> 00:04:06.566
the modern-day Republican

00:04:06.934 --> 00:04:07.934
party.

00:04:08.168 --> 00:04:08.936
So, there's this question --

00:04:09.202 --> 00:04:10.137
right, Jim?

00:04:10.404 --> 00:04:10.437
--

00:04:10.938 --> 00:04:12.205
of what is it mean to be a

00:04:12.472 --> 00:04:13.373
conservative over the next four

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:13.740
years.

00:04:14.007 --> 00:04:14.374
Who is going to be the

00:04:14.942 --> 00:04:15.442
individual or individuals to

00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:18.178
take that mantle in the charge

00:04:18.445 --> 00:04:21.181
of defining what conservative

00:04:21.448 --> 00:04:22.249
policies and positions would be

00:04:22.516 --> 00:04:23.550
as far as legislation goes.

00:04:23.817 --> 00:04:25.319
And I don't think the party has

00:04:25.585 --> 00:04:26.386
quite figured that out.

00:04:27.254 --> 00:04:29.289
CPAC has had many symptoms CPAC

00:04:29.556 --> 00:04:29.690
version.

00:04:30.157 --> 00:04:30.590
Florida, many other states

00:04:30.958 --> 00:04:31.525
across the country, for two

00:04:32.192 --> 00:04:32.392
years now --

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:33.560
and if you look at the crowd,

00:04:33.827 --> 00:04:34.428
it is a very low.

00:04:35.195 --> 00:04:35.595
It's a different crowd.

00:04:35.862 --> 00:04:36.596
And I think that's indicative

00:04:36.964 --> 00:04:38.332
of the fact that many in the

00:04:38.598 --> 00:04:40.233
party are trying to figure out,

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:41.501
where do we go after trump?

00:04:41.768 --> 00:04:42.469
Do we stick with him?

00:04:43.203 --> 00:04:44.271
Or is it time to move on.

00:04:45.172 --> 00:04:45.539
And as a strategist --

00:04:46.239 --> 00:04:47.174
and you are looking at the

00:04:47.441 --> 00:04:47.607
numbers --

00:04:47.874 --> 00:04:49.276
I would probably say, Jim, that

00:04:49.543 --> 00:04:50.377
it is time to move in a new

00:04:50.644 --> 00:04:53.180
direction where the party risks

00:04:53.447 --> 00:04:54.247
a repeat of trump versus Biden

00:04:54.514 --> 00:04:54.648
in 2024.

00:04:55.983 --> 00:04:56.083
Mp.

00:04:56.984 --> 00:04:59.820
&gt;&gt; Right, 2018, 2020, 2022.

00:05:00.988 --> 00:05:02.990
It's not exactly a winning

00:05:03.123 --> 00:05:03.256
streak.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:04.992
Molly, one thing we also

00:05:04.992 --> 00:05:06.994
noticed this week was Nikki

00:05:07.127 --> 00:05:08.996
Haley, trump's former U.S.

00:05:09.096 --> 00:05:09.830
Ambassador to the when --

00:05:10.097 --> 00:05:10.998
she had sort of chilly

00:05:11.231 --> 00:05:13.700
reception at times.

00:05:13.967 --> 00:05:15.902
Mike Pompeo, he was talking

00:05:16.169 --> 00:05:17.704
about the former presidents

00:05:17.971 --> 00:05:18.538
veiled references.

00:05:19.006 --> 00:05:19.172
Let's listen to that.

00:05:20.507 --> 00:05:23.143
Nikki&gt;&gt; We have lost the popular

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:25.112
vote in the last seven out of

00:05:25.379 --> 00:05:26.346
eight presidential elections.

00:05:27.114 --> 00:05:28.281
Our cause is right.

00:05:29.116 --> 00:05:30.250
But we have failed to win the

00:05:30.517 --> 00:05:32.119
confidence of a majority of

00:05:32.386 --> 00:05:33.153
Americans.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:34.287
That ends now.

00:05:35.155 --> 00:05:38.191
&gt;&gt; Losing is bad because losing

00:05:38.458 --> 00:05:39.159
is bad.

00:05:39.426 --> 00:05:40.427
But the principles that we

00:05:40.694 --> 00:05:42.229
stand for our what really is at

00:05:42.496 --> 00:05:42.596
risk.

00:05:43.263 --> 00:05:44.097
And it's not a political

00:05:44.364 --> 00:05:44.498
problem.

00:05:45.132 --> 00:05:48.201
The problem is that the losses

00:05:48.468 --> 00:05:50.203
are a symptom of something much

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:50.604
bigger.

00:05:51.138 --> 00:05:53.073
I think it's a crisis in

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:53.573
conservatism.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.275
We have lost confidence that we

00:05:55.542 --> 00:05:56.209
are right.

00:05:57.044 --> 00:05:59.112
It's not just a crisis of

00:05:59.379 --> 00:05:59.579
confidence.

00:05:59.846 --> 00:06:02.115
It's one of character as well.

00:06:02.382 --> 00:06:05.118
&gt;&gt; Molly, this is almost what

00:06:05.385 --> 00:06:06.319
shermichael we're seeing a few

00:06:06.586 --> 00:06:07.187
moments ago.

00:06:07.454 --> 00:06:09.189
The message for some of these

00:06:09.456 --> 00:06:10.157
candidates is that they want to

00:06:10.424 --> 00:06:11.258
move on from trump they don't

00:06:11.525 --> 00:06:13.160
want to say his name in any of

00:06:13.427 --> 00:06:13.660
the speeches.

00:06:14.094 --> 00:06:15.228
It's almost like beetle juice.

00:06:15.495 --> 00:06:16.363
They don't want to say because

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:18.265
use too many times.

00:06:19.132 --> 00:06:22.202
What is going on there with

00:06:22.469 --> 00:06:24.171
some of these candidates like

00:06:24.438 --> 00:06:26.106
Nikki Haley and Mike Pompeo are

00:06:26.373 --> 00:06:28.108
just sort of tiptoeing around

00:06:28.375 --> 00:06:28.475
trump?

00:06:28.742 --> 00:06:31.111
&gt;&gt; I think that they could

00:06:31.378 --> 00:06:33.146
quietly lull away the base by

00:06:33.413 --> 00:06:36.083
sort of tricking them into not

00:06:36.349 --> 00:06:37.217
saying anything mean about

00:06:37.484 --> 00:06:40.120
trump but offering them sort of

00:06:40.387 --> 00:06:43.090
trumpism light as a kind of

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:44.191
possible bait and switch.

00:06:44.458 --> 00:06:46.193
And I think that, ultimately,

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:47.994
this stems from this idea that

00:06:48.261 --> 00:06:49.129
they will not take the pain.

00:06:49.396 --> 00:06:50.263
They want the fringe.

00:06:51.098 --> 00:06:53.200
They want those votes because

00:06:53.467 --> 00:06:55.969
trump won in 2016 by never

00:06:56.236 --> 00:06:58.038
alienating alienating the

00:06:58.305 --> 00:06:58.438
fringe.

00:06:59.106 --> 00:07:01.174
And that fringe is really very

00:07:01.441 --> 00:07:02.008
fringy.

00:07:02.275 --> 00:07:03.210
That's the white nationalists

00:07:03.477 --> 00:07:05.178
and the autocrats and really

00:07:05.445 --> 00:07:07.114
they're just the sort of drags.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:09.116
And those were the people we

00:07:09.282 --> 00:07:10.083
are, for such a long time,

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:11.251
Republicans rejected those

00:07:11.518 --> 00:07:11.651
people.

00:07:12.119 --> 00:07:14.121
And trump knows that is a good

00:07:14.387 --> 00:07:15.122
amount of people.

00:07:15.288 --> 00:07:16.123
And that's how he won.

00:07:17.124 --> 00:07:19.126
I mean, it's a good quantity of

00:07:19.326 --> 00:07:20.127
people, not good people.

00:07:21.128 --> 00:07:22.996
So, I do think, ultimately,

00:07:23.263 --> 00:07:24.097
they are trying to get those

00:07:24.364 --> 00:07:25.132
people without getting those

00:07:25.365 --> 00:07:27.134
people and without alienating

00:07:27.367 --> 00:07:27.467
them.

00:07:27.734 --> 00:07:29.136
And I think, ultimately,

00:07:29.302 --> 00:07:30.203
someone is going to have to say,

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:31.338
like, we don't embrace this

00:07:31.605 --> 00:07:32.139
kind of racism.

00:07:32.405 --> 00:07:33.273
And they are going to have to

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:34.040
take the pain.

00:07:34.307 --> 00:07:36.143
And then, eventually, rebuild

00:07:36.409 --> 00:07:36.576
the party.

00:07:37.144 --> 00:07:38.078
&gt;&gt; Shermichael singleton, what

00:07:38.345 --> 00:07:39.146
do you think about that?

00:07:39.346 --> 00:07:40.313
Can you take on trump without

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:43.150
really confronting him by name

00:07:43.283 --> 00:07:44.117
or confronting him in general?

00:07:44.384 --> 00:07:46.052
What would you advise as a

00:07:46.319 --> 00:07:48.088
Republican strategist?

00:07:48.355 --> 00:07:49.990
&gt;&gt; I mean, look.

00:07:50.257 --> 00:07:51.158
You have to.

00:07:51.291 --> 00:07:53.160
You clearly would risk not

00:07:53.393 --> 00:07:55.228
being a competitive individual

00:07:55.495 --> 00:07:57.164
not running for the Republican

00:07:57.230 --> 00:07:57.397
nominee --

00:07:57.998 --> 00:07:59.166
for nomination, I should say --

00:07:59.366 --> 00:08:00.033
but you have to.

00:08:01.168 --> 00:08:02.102
Pompeo is right where he argues

00:08:02.369 --> 00:08:03.270
or made the statement about

00:08:03.537 --> 00:08:03.770
conservatism.

00:08:04.037 --> 00:08:05.172
I think the problem I have --

00:08:05.438 --> 00:08:07.174
and I don't want to get

00:08:07.307 --> 00:08:07.641
philosophical here.

00:08:07.974 --> 00:08:09.276
But the party has really become

00:08:09.543 --> 00:08:11.178
a sort of nationalist populist

00:08:11.411 --> 00:08:11.511
party.

00:08:11.978 --> 00:08:13.180
And I think there is room

00:08:13.346 --> 00:08:16.049
within the American populist

00:08:16.316 --> 00:08:17.184
generally speaking for

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:18.285
conservatism in the traditional

00:08:18.552 --> 00:08:20.120
and classical sense.

00:08:20.387 --> 00:08:22.155
But I think that's far removed.

00:08:22.422 --> 00:08:23.190
When you look at many of the

00:08:23.456 --> 00:08:25.992
struggles out of more diverse

00:08:26.259 --> 00:08:27.194
